sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-51> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Sophisticated computation, or primes, are not signs of intelligence" ;
    dct:description "Producing a signal by sophisticated computation is no evidence of intelligence, because such computation is common in ordinary physical processes; for instance, turbulence in the gas around a star quickly does more computation than all of human intellectual history, and sequences such as the primes can be generated by many non-intelligent systems." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Intelligence in the Universe” (from p. 822), p. 837. Scope: criteria for detecting extraterrestrial intelligence; natural physical processes" ;
    dct:type "general-principle" .
}

